{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"47664ced13b4a228","name":"card_event_screening_force","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/WorldAtWar/events/card_event_screening_force","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SCREENING FORCE","text":"Costs 1 less for each friendly BREWSTER F2A. Add two SBD 3 DAUNTLESS with Blitz to your support line.","flavorText":"During WWII, the concept of an aerial screening force became vital for the protection of bomber formations and advancing ground troops. The US employed dedicated fighter units to form protective barriers against enemy aircraft, especially over Europe where the skies were heavily contested. Escort fighters like the P-47 Thunderbolt and P-51 Mustang flew ahead or alongside bomber groups, intercepting German fighters before they could close in on the main formation. The US Ninth Air Force, operating in support of Allied ground advances, often coordinated with the British Second Tactical Air Force to neutralize enemy air threats. These formations patrolled likely approach routes, engaged enemy aircraft before they reached the front lines, and maintained air superiority over the battlefield. Screening extended beyond physical interception, with electronic warfare also playing a role. US aircraft equipped with jamming devices disrupted German radar and communications during large operations, such as the Normandy landings. These efforts prevented enemy fighters and Flak batteries from responding effectively. Screening forces, both physical and electronic, were essential to the success of Allied air and ground operations.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":7,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_screening_force","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_screening_force_title","SourceString":"SCREENING FORCE","LocalizedString":"SCREENING FORCE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":7,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_screening_force_text","SourceString":"Costs 1 less for each friendly BREWSTER F2A. Add two SBD 3 DAUNTLESS with Blitz to your support line.","LocalizedString":"Costs 1 less for each friendly BREWSTER F2A. Add two SBD 3 DAUNTLESS with Blitz to your support line."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_screening_force_history","SourceString":"During WWII, the concept of an aerial screening force became vital for the protection of bomber formations and advancing ground troops. The US employed dedicated fighter units to form protective barriers against enemy aircraft, especially over Europe where the skies were heavily contested. Escort fighters like the P-47 Thunderbolt and P-51 Mustang flew ahead or alongside bomber groups, intercepting German fighters before they could close in on the main formation. The US Ninth Air Force, operating in support of Allied ground advances, often coordinated with the British Second Tactical Air Force to neutralize enemy air threats. These formations patrolled likely approach routes, engaged enemy aircraft before they reached the front lines, and maintained air superiority over the battlefield. Screening extended beyond physical interception, with electronic warfare also playing a role. US aircraft equipped with jamming devices disrupted German radar and communications during large operations, such as the Normandy landings. These efforts prevented enemy fighters and Flak batteries from responding effectively. Screening forces, both physical and electronic, were essential to the success of Allied air and ground operations.","LocalizedString":"During WWII, the concept of an aerial screening force became vital for the protection of bomber formations and advancing ground troops. The US employed dedicated fighter units to form protective barriers against enemy aircraft, especially over Europe where the skies were heavily contested. Escort fighters like the P-47 Thunderbolt and P-51 Mustang flew ahead or alongside bomber groups, intercepting German fighters before they could close in on the main formation. The US Ninth Air Force, operating in support of Allied ground advances, often coordinated with the British Second Tactical Air Force to neutralize enemy air threats. These formations patrolled likely approach routes, engaged enemy aircraft before they reached the front lines, and maintained air superiority over the battlefield. Screening extended beyond physical interception, with electronic warfare also playing a role. US aircraft equipped with jamming devices disrupted German radar and communications during large operations, such as the Normandy landings. These efforts prevented enemy fighters and Flak batteries from responding effectively. Screening forces, both physical and electronic, were essential to the success of Allied air and ground operations."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardEnterPlay","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardLeaveBoardOrOwner"],"spawnCardName":"card_unit_sbd3_dauntless","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/USA/World_at_War/T_screening_force.T_screening_force","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/SCREENING_FORCE.SCREENING_FORCE","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":397.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_screening_force_photo","SourceString":"An escort group of four US Army Air Force P-51 Mustang fighters in 1944.","LocalizedString":"An escort group of four US Army Air Force P-51 Mustang fighters in 1944."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"isAttackBuff":true,"isDefenseBu